A||N||3||jasonmaddox||86 2.8L Problems with timing and Altern.||||5||
Z||000000||jasonmaddox||12-12-2001||12:58 PM||||I'm having two problems with my car, one is with Timing, the car runs fine and I do not see any glowing manifolds, however how do I know if the distributor in the right slot/tooth? I'm asking because the car turns over much slower when hot.<P>Two, I had my alternator tested, and it's suppose to be okay. However as the car heats up the Volts gauge goes from 15-16 when cold to 13-14 when hot. As you turn on the headlights, blower motor and wipers. It dips down under 13. Is this normal? I seem to remember it always staying above 14 or 15.<P>Thanks,<BR>Jason A. Maddox||||reg||5
Z||000001||Voytek||12-12-2001||01:11 PM||||Welcome.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>#1. How do you define 'much slower'.  For example, the Fiero will run at a high RPM for a while until the temperature starts rising.  Then the RPM drops to normal (should be around 900 at idle, I believe).  This is perfectly normal.<P>As far as knowing if your distributor is in the right orientation - have you done anything to it recently?  One way is to take it to a shop.  They will set the correct timing with a strobelight.  I've had this done recently for $20 CDN - car runs perfect.<P>#2.  I'm not sure if it's normal or not but mine does the same.  Higher voltage when it's cold and lower when it warms up.  Does the increase / decrease correspond to the high and low RPM?  If so, then it IS perfectly normal.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||jasonmaddox||12-12-2001||01:20 PM||||1) I didn't explain enough in the last message, sorry. I meant it turns over slower when hot when attempting to start it. (Cranking via starter) <P>2) I replaced the motor, and the stobelight shows it's on 11 degrees BTC. If the distributor is in the wrong place, will this number always be wrong?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/86se1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-12-2001||02:24 PM||||First ?,<BR>Sounds like you have a starter solenoid going bad. Is there a heat shield installed around it?<P>Second ?,<BR>As the car warms, it typically runs at a 'higher than normal' idle speed that should lie between 1200 & 1500 RPMs, thus a higher voltage reading could be considered 'normal'.<BR>As the load increases, (by turning stuff on) the output of the alternator may indicate a slight decrease because of the higher demand. If it did not do this prior to the rebuild, you may have missed a ground or not adjusted the belt correctly.<P>(I forgot this!)<P>The timing should be close enough, but you may wish to adjust it just a tad! If further rotation of the distributor cannot be found, remove, rotate to next tooth, in the correct direction and re-install it. Then re-set your engine timing<P>My $0.02 worth!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
